bles (NCJ 304939). Bureau of Justice Statistics. \nhttps://bjs.ojp.gov/content/pub/pdf/fdcda20st.pdf \nBureau of Justice Statistics. (2021). Use of force. U.S. Department of Justice, Office of Justice \nPrograms. https://bjs.ojp.gov/topics/use-of-force \nBureau of Justice Statistics. (2022). Death in custody reporting act. U.S. Department of Justice, \nOffice of Justice Programs. https://bja.ojp.gov/funding/performance-measures/DCRA- \nReporting-Guidance-FAQs.pdf \nCahill, M., Labrida, M., & Taylor, J. (2022). Police killings: Road map of research priorities for \nchange. RAND. \nCalifornia Community Colleges. (2022). The Modern Policing Degree task force. \nhttps://www.cccco.edu/About-Us/Chancellors-Office/Divisions/Workforce-and- \nEconomic-Development/modern-policing-degree-task-force \nCarmichael, J. T., & Kent, S. L. (2015). The use of lethal force by Canadian police officers: \nAssessing the influence of female police officers and minority threat explanations on \npolice shootings across large cities. American Journal of Criminal Justice, 40, 703–721. \nCenter for Behavioral Health Statistics and Quality. (2021). 2020 National Survey on Drug Use \nand Health Public Use File Codebook. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services \nAdministration. \nCity of Durham. (n.d.). Community safety. https://www.durhamnc.gov/4576/Community-Safety \nCohen, G., & Bagwell, M. (2023). The state of mental health training in basic police curricula: A \nnational level examination. Journal of Public Affairs Education, 29(3), 327–349. \nCohen, L. E., & Felson, M. (1979). Social change and crime rate trends: A routine activity \napproach. American Sociological Review, 44(4), 588–608. \nColorado Health Institute. (2020). Responding to behavioral health needs: An evaluation of the \nColorado Office of Behavioral Health’s Co-Responder Program. \nhttps://www.opioidlibrary.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/OBH-CoResponder- \nFINAL.pdf \nCook, R., Summers, D., Paulk, R., & Kirsch, E. (2022). 